# Migrations that are deliberately NOT applied to production, one id per line.
#
# The bundle stamps how many migrations it expects, and the drift banner fires
# when production has fewer. Counting a migration that is intentionally on the
# shelf makes that warning permanent — and a warning that is always on is one
# nobody reads on the day it is finally true.
#
# They stay in this directory on purpose: tests/database.mjs enumerates the
# folder, and that is the only thing exercising them before they go live.
#
# Remove the line in the same change that applies the migration.

# 202609140034 puts two AFTER triggers on `messages` — the hottest write path in
# the app — and starts recording a durable, personal record of a pair that
# nothing previously kept. Both halves want a human read before they are live,
# the same call 202609070011 makes about money: the triggers because a mistake
# there is felt on every send, and the recording because an opt-in that begins
# collecting is not something to switch on for somebody else.
#
# The client is safe in the meantime and does not need this migration to work.
# together_timeline() and set_together_optin() keep their existing signatures, so
# an un-migrated production returns the derived half of the timeline exactly as
# it does today; the filter chips simply find fewer groups, and together_status()
# answers without event_count, which the client reads as "unknown" and phrases
# around rather than rendering as a confident zero.
#
# Applying it: run the migration in the SQL editor, then
# supabase/operations/schedule_together_purge.sql standalone (cron statements
# have aborted batched transactions on some projects), then delete these lines.
#
# Scheduled messages. Shelved on arrival, not because it is unfinished but
# because applying it is a decision about retention: it is the one table in
# this app that holds a message body in plaintext on purpose, for up to a week.
# Apply 202609150040 in the SQL editor, then operations/schedule_scheduled_messages.sql
# standalone (cron statements have aborted batched transactions), and remove the
# line below in the SAME change. Without the cron job the rows accumulate and
# nothing ever sends.
#
# The client half ships either way and hides itself: loadScheduled() tells a
# missing RPC apart from a failed one, so on a database without this migration
# the clock button and the strip simply do not render.
# 202609140037 adds the deletion grace period and two tables to the export.
# Shelved because it has not been applied by hand yet and must not be counted
# against production until it is: AccountData falls back to the immediate
# delete when account_deletion_state() answers PGRST202, so the screen is
# correct either way. Apply it, schedule operations/schedule_account_purge.sql
# separately, then remove this line.
# Operational telemetry. Shelved deliberately, not forgotten: it stands up a NEW
# metadata store on a production database with three real users, where the
# honest answer is that hour buckets and missing identifier columns reduce
# linkage but cannot deliver anonymity at that population. That is the owner's
# call to make with the schema in front of them, not a side effect of a deploy.
# Nothing in the app depends on it — lib/telemetry.js disables itself for the
# session the first time the RPC answers PGRST202 — so the frontend ships safely
# ahead of it either way.
202609140035_ops_telemetry

# 202609150041 makes the database emit a kind the live bundle renders as a
# bogus "Photo" tile. It goes live only AFTER the frontend carrying the thread
# event ships and is verified on the served bundle.

# 202609150060 is shelved ONLY because it is blocked on 202609140034, which is
# itself on this list. It alters together_events (a new `game_result` kind) and
# derives the pair's lifetime game record from it, so a database without 0034
# has no table to alter — the migration raises
#
#   together_events is missing. Apply 202609140034_timeline_events first.
#
# rather than letting "relation does not exist" be the error an operator has to
# interpret. Apply the two in one sitting, 0034 first, and remove BOTH lines in
# that same change. There is nothing else holding it back: it adds no trigger to
# a hot path, no new table, and no write a client can reach.
#
# The client half ships either way. loadGameRecord() tells a missing RPC apart
# from a failed read (lib/gameRecord.js), so on a database without this the
# Games tab is simply not drawn — and, crucially, a read that merely FAILED
# still draws it and says so, because a section that hides itself on a flaky
# network is a failure rendered as an answer.

# 202609150090 publishes E2E identity PUBLIC keys. Stage 2 of 4 and completely
# inert: no client reads or writes it yet, and media is still stored exactly as
# it is today. It is listed here only because it has not been applied by hand.
#
# Applying it EARLY is deliberate rather than premature — keys have to exist and
# be readable for a while before the first encrypted object is sent, or the
# first send has nobody to wrap a content key for. Apply it in the SQL editor
# and delete these lines in the same change.
202609150090_e2e_keys
